WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.934
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.092 --> 00:00:06.550
SPEAKER: Do you
have an application

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:09.370
with global reach,
one with millions

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.460
or even billions of users
who need highly accurate,

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.390
real-time information?

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.230
The database that sits behind
your application and powers

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.830
your users can dictate your
users' experience, your ability

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.170
to grow, and your
global resilience.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.300
Cloud Spanner is a strongly
consistent, highly scalable

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.170
relational database that is
fully managed by Google Cloud.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.590
So how is Cloud
Spanner so powerful?

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.530
Well, it creates multiple
copies, or replicas,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.020
of your data, and
stores these replicas

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.480
across geographical locations.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.570
For enterprises with
globally distributed users,

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.730
a multiregion
configuration is ideal,

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.010
as it protects against
regional failures,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.380
enables you to
scale to new places,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.390
and provides faster
reads by having

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.060
a copy of your database
near your users.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.710
Let's unpack how it works.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.590
For multiregion
configurations, Cloud Spanner

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.840
creates five
replicas of your data

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.840
and distributes them
across three regions.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.570
One region is the
leader region, which

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.450
is responsible for
handling writes.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.360
The leader logs the write and
sends it to other replicas.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.130
Any write to the
database requires

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:23.700
a quorum, or agreement,
between at least two regions.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.250
This ensures that
if a region fails,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.900
there are at least
two regions that

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.780
can form a quorum with full,
up-to-date copies of your data,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.160
so your database can continue
to be available without any data

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:35.980
loss.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.400
So what happens if the
leader region fails?

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.680
Cloud Spanner is
built to be resilient

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.720
and moves the leader
to another region.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.210
The new leader region and
the other available region

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.260
form the quorum.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.080
This means the database
continues to be available.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.450
Your application might see
slightly higher latencies

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.110
during the leader movement, but
won't experience any downtime.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.240
To scale your application
across the world,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.540
you can use read-only
regions close to your users.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.620
Reads in Cloud Spanner will, by
default, read the latest data.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.360
These are called
strong reads and are

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.539
served by a replica
closest to the user.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:12.090
If the read is in a different
region from the leader,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.550
the replica may have to
communicate with the leader

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.660
to know whether it
has the latest data,

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.450
and this can increase latency.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.480
To get faster reads,
you can use stale reads.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.690
Stale reads are done at
a timestamp in the past--

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.030
for example, 15
seconds in the past--

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.180
and are useful if you can
tolerate slightly older data.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.210
Businesses around the world
are powering their users

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.370
with Cloud Spanner.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.460
A multiregion configuration
gives you the peace of mind

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.790
that you have high availability,
low latency, and resilience

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.830
against regional failures.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.070
To find out more, check out
the Cloud Spanner website.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

